  "texts": [
    "I'll start by exploring the workspace to find the relevant source file.",
    "I re-read the source (it had concurrent modifications), located the slice logic, and submitted a fresh patch.\n\n**Root cause** (`more_itertools/more.py`, `peekable._get_slice`): the cache-fill decision was `if (start < 0) or (stop < 0): self._cache.extend(self._it)`. For a negative-step slice with an *omitted* stop, `stop` is normalized to `-maxsize - 1`, which is negative, so the whole source was drained even though the reverse slice can never reach past `start`. Hence a `p[5::-1]` on a 100-item (or infinite) source read all 100 items, and sources that stop being readable at the reverse boundary blew up.\n\n**Fix**: detect `reverse_to_start = (step < 0) and (index.stop is None) and (start >= 0)`. In that case the boundary is \"before element 0\" for any iterable length, so only `start + 1` ite"
  ],
